<file path=".serena/memories/memory_maintenance.md">
# Memory Maintenance

## Discovery Model

- Core principle: progressive discovery through references, building a graph of memories.
- Initially, agents are provided with the list of all memories (names only).
- Agents should read `mem:core` as the top-level entry point (graph root).
  This memory should contain references to other memories covering major project domains.
  The referenced memories shall, in turn, shall contain references to even more specific memories, and so on.
  The depth of the graph shall depend on the project complexity.
- Use topics/folders to group related memories in order to make the content structure explicit.
  Folders can mirror project structure (e.g. modules like frontend/backend) or topics like debugging, architecture, etc.
- Memory references must use a mem: prefix inside backticks, e.g. `mem:frontend/core`.
  The surrounding text should clearly indicate when to read the memory/which content to expect.
  The text should provide more precise guidance than the memory name alone,
  i.e. avoid a reference like "frontend debugging: `mem:frontend/debugging` and instead make clear which aspects of frontend debugging are covered.
- Memories themselves should not contain information about when to read them; this is the responsibility of the referring memory.

## Style

Dense agent notes, not prose docs. Prefer invariants, terse bullets.
Avoid obvious context, rationale, and examples unless they prevent likely mistakes.
Keep guidance durable and generalizable, not task-local.

## Add/update threshold

Add or update memories only with stable, non-obvious project conventions that avoid complex rediscovery in the future.
Do not add: quick-read facts; generic language/framework knowledge; one-off task notes; volatile line-level details; behavior likely to change soon.

## Maintenance Actions

- Renaming memories: References are updated automatically if handled via Serena's memory rename tool.
- Checking for stale memories (e.g. after deletion): Call `serena memories check` for a report.
</file>

<file path=".serena/memories/suggested_commands.md">
# Suggested Commands

Run from repo root unless noted.

## Test
- `melos run test` — full suite (script tests + dart tests + flutter tests)
- `melos run test:dart` — non-Flutter dart tests only
- `melos run test:flutter` — Flutter tests (serial, `--no-pub`)
- `melos exec -- "dart test"` — single package tests (cd to package first, or use `melos exec --scope`)

## Lint / Analyze
- `melos run analyze` — `dart analyze .` across all packages

## Hygiene (run before PR)
- `melos run boundary-check` — import boundary enforcement
- `melos run source-text` — scaffold text hygiene
- `melos run dependency-audit` — dependency version report (non-mutating)

## Individual script tests
- `melos run boundary-check:test`
- `melos run dependency-audit:test`
- `melos run source-text:test`

## Windows notes
- Use forward slashes in paths (bash shell)
- Python scripts require `python` (not `python3`)
- Flutter tests are serial (`--concurrency=1`)

## RTK
- Preferred: `rtk melos run test`, `rtk dart analyze`, `rtk dart test`
- RTK transparently proxies these — identical flags/behavior
</file>

<file path=".serena/memories/task_completion.md">
# Task Completion Checklist

Run these from repo root when a coding task is considered done. All must pass.

## Required gates
1. `melos run analyze` — static analysis across all packages
2. `melos run boundary-check` — import boundary enforcement
3. `melos run source-text` — scaffold text hygiene
4. `melos run test` — full test suite (script + dart + flutter)

## Per-package quick iteration
- `melos exec --scope <package> -- "dart test"` — single package tests
- `melos exec --scope <package> -- "dart analyze ."` — single package analysis

## If golden files changed
- Confirm semantic change is intended
- Version notes exist where needed
- Invariants still hold (per `docs/INVARIANTS.md`)

## If new package dependency added
- Confirm it's declared in the importing package's pubspec
- Confirm no `src/` boundary violation
- `melos run boundary-check` must pass

## Before push
All four required gates must be green. Any failure is a blocker.
</file>

<file path=".serena/memories/tech_stack.md">
# Tech Stack

## Runtime
- **Language**: Dart (SDK `^3.11.5`)
- **Apps**: Flutter (mobile + desktop)

## Monorepo tooling
- **Package management**: Dart pub workspace (root `pubspec.yaml` workspace section)
- **Orchestration**: Melos `^7.8.1` (scripts in `melos.yaml` inside root pubspec)
- **Lint**: `package:lints/recommended.yaml` + project rules (single quotes, no print, directive ordering)

## Packages (15)
All under `packages/`: protocol, core, variants, modes, crypto, network, sync, replay, receipts, capture, privacy, wizard, ui\_kit, native\_bridges, testkit

## Apps (2)
`apps/peerdeal_mobile`, `apps/peerdeal_desktop` — both Flutter

## Scripts (Python)
- `scripts/check_package_boundaries.py` — import boundary enforcement
- `scripts/dependency_audit.py` — dependency version audit
- `scripts/check_source_text.py` — checked-in scaffold text hygiene

## Testing
- Pure Dart: `dart test`
- Flutter: `flutter test --no-pub`
- Script tests: Python unittest in `scripts/test_*.py`
- Orchestrated via `melos run test`

## Key dependencies by package
- `peerdeal_protocol`: zero PeerDeal deps (foundation)
- `peerdeal_core`: depends on protocol only
- `peerdeal_variants`, `peerdeal_modes`: protocol + core
- Apps depend on package public APIs only
</file>

